Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/4] accel/rocket: add support for the RK3576 NPU

This adds RK3576 support to the rocket driver: binding, driver match
data, and the SoC dtsi nodes.

The RK3576 carries the same RKNN IP as the RK3588 with two cores
instead of three. Three things differ and are handled here:

 - Job completion cannot be delivered as an interrupt: the PC_DONE
   bits in INTERRUPT_MASK are read-only on this SoC and
   OPERATION_ENABLE does not clear on completion. Patch 3 adds per-SoC
   match data and polls INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS for the PC_DONE bits from
   a 1 ms hrtimer on affected SoCs. This matches the "job timeouts
   followed by a full SoC lock-up" Nicolas reported on RK3576 during
   the v2 review of rocket [1].

 - There is no NPU SRAM rail, so patch 1 makes sram-supply conditional
   in the binding.

 - The clk_bulk array in rocket_core_init() never sets .id, so every
   entry resolves to the node's first clock. Harmless on RK3588 as
   things stand, fatal on RK3576 where hclk stays gated. Patch 2 looks
   the clocks up by the names the binding has always required.

The register layout in patch 4 (per-core pc/cna/core split with the
IOMMU inside the hole at core base + 0x2000 — the layout that led to
the reg split discussion during review [2][3]) is cross-checked
against the vendor BSP devicetree, and core 0 is confirmed on
hardware: Jiaxing Hu has MobileNetV1 running through Mesa/Teflon on a
Radxa ROCK 4D with an equivalent out-of-tree series [4]. This series
is an independent implementation written against mainline, with the
completion-polling approach and the clock findings credited to that
bring-up work.

Marked RFC because I could only compile-test: I don't have RK3576
hardware. Build coverage: rocket builds clean as a module with W=1 on
arm64 defconfig, all ten rk3576 board dtbs build without new warnings,
and dt_binding_check / CHECK_DTBS pass on the touched schema (including
a negative test that sram-supply is now rejected for the RK3576
compatible).

Known open items, deliberately not in this series:

 - The rknn nodes stay disabled. Enabling a core today requires
   keeping the NPU rail always on, because the power-domain chain
   status on the NPU hierarchy never asserts after power-off and
   rockchip-pm-domain then cannot power-cycle it. That needs either a
   pmdomain fix or domain-supply support, and is being investigated by
   the people with hardware [4]. Board enablement should follow once
   that is settled.
 - Whether the RKNN BIU resets want to be cycled by the power domain
   rather than held by the core driver is part of the same discussion.
 - The boot firmware can leave an orphaned page fault on IOMMU bank 0,
   which currently trips rk_iommu's stall handling; fixes for that are
   in [4] and belong to the IOMMU driver, not here.

TODO (TRM/hardware access needed): the OPP table / clock rates for the
cores are not included; the vendor tree programs CLK_RKNN_DSU0 via SCMI
with board-fused voltages, and I did not want to guess at frequencies
that nobody can validate right now.

Tested on: nothing physical, see above. If you have a ROCK 4D, Sige5
or NanoPi M5, testing reports are very welcome.

